WTF: RedBlackTree::remove clears the links of the node it removes.

A node's links to its children are CheckedPtrs, and remove() left the removed node's m_left and m_right
as they were. A node taken out of the tree then still counted as a holder of its old children: when one
of them was destroyed first, WTF_OVERRIDE_DELETE_FOR_CHECKED_PTR found a count above zero and turned it
into a zeroed zombie, and destroying the removed node afterwards (or re-inserting it, which resets its
links) decremented the zombie's zero count and crashed (crashDueToCheckedPtrToDeadObject). The generic
RunLoop, the one this port runs, keeps its timers in such a tree: stopping timers and destroying them in
another order is enough (one-shot timers started, stopped and destroyed newest first: an abort every
time on the build host, `webkit-views timers`). webd aborted that way when a web process's proxy was torn down
(~ResponsivenessTimer -> ~TimerBase, and RedBlackTree::insert's depth check from ResponsivenessTimer::start
under WKPageGoBack), seen in the browser monkey's runs right after a process swap; it is likely what
patches 0021 and 0027 work around. The removed node is now reset, so it holds nothing.

--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/RedBlackTree.h
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/RedBlackTree.h
@@ -285,6 +285,10 @@
             }
         } else if (y->color() == Black)
             removeFixup(x, xParent);
 
+        // LazyOS: out of the tree, the node holds no CheckedPtr to its old children (one of them may be
+        // destroyed before it, and its count must not still include this node).
+        z->reset();
+
         return z;
     }
